# 3. Token Architecture

GrowFlow uses three conceptual token layers.

```text
PRIMITIVE TOKENS
    ↓
SEMANTIC TOKENS
    ↓
COMPONENT TOKENS
```

## 3.1 Primitive Tokens

Primitive tokens describe raw design values.

Examples:

```text
color.green.600
color.gray.900
space.4
radius.md
font.weight.semibold
duration.fast
```

Primitive tokens should generally not be consumed directly by application pages.

## 3.2 Semantic Tokens

Semantic tokens describe meaning.

Examples:

```text
color.background.canvas
color.surface.default
color.text.primary
color.border.subtle
color.action.primary
color.status.success
color.ai.surface
```

Pages and most components should consume semantic tokens.

## 3.3 Component Tokens

Component tokens describe a reusable component's visual contract.

Examples:

```text
button.primary.background
button.primary.backgroundHover
input.border
table.header.background
sidebar.item.activeBackground
ai.message.surface
```

Component tokens may reference semantic tokens.

---

# 4. Naming Convention

Canonical naming structure:

```text
<category>.<role>.<property>.<state>
```

Examples:

```text
color.text.primary
color.surface.default
color.action.primary.hover
button.primary.background
button.primary.backgroundHover
input.border.focus
```

For CSS variables:

```text
--gf-color-text-primary
--gf-color-surface-default
--gf-button-primary-background
```

For TypeScript:

```text
tokens.color.text.primary
tokens.color.surface.default
tokens.button.primary.background
```

## 4.1 Naming Rules

Use:

- semantic meaning
- stable role names
- predictable hierarchy
- explicit state names

Avoid:

```text
greenButton
niceGray
homepageGreen
dashboardCardColor
darkGray2
randomBorder
```

Prefer:

```text
color.action.primary
color.surface.secondary
color.border.standard
```

