# 29. Motion Tokens

Motion follows:

> **Float → Settle**

Motion should feel like subtle physical movement.

## Durations

Recommended:

```text
duration.instant = 0ms
duration.fast = 120ms
duration.standard = 180ms
duration.moderate = 240ms
duration.slow = 320ms
duration.emphasis = 420ms
```

Avoid long decorative animations.

---

# 30. Easing Tokens

Recommended:

```text
easing.standard
easing.enter
easing.exit
easing.emphasis
```

Conceptually:

- standard: smooth general transition
- enter: slightly softer entrance
- exit: efficient exit
- emphasis: controlled physical settling

Exact cubic-bezier implementation may be standardized at component-system implementation time, but individual pages must not invent unrelated motion curves.

---

# 31. Motion Usage

Appropriate:

- hover transitions
- dropdown opening
- drawer movement
- modal entrance
- progress transitions
- page element settling
- notification appearance
- AI state transitions

Avoid:

- constant background movement
- excessive parallax
- perpetual floating elements
- fake AI typing
- animated charts without informational purpose
- decorative motion that competes with task completion

---

# 32. Reduced Motion

When reduced motion is enabled:

- remove nonessential movement
- reduce transition distance
- reduce duration
- disable decorative animations
- preserve functional state changes through opacity, instant state changes, or static indicators

Accessibility preference always overrides decorative motion.

---

# 33. Interaction State Tokens

Every interactive component should define:

```text
default
hover
active
focus
disabled
loading
selected
error
success
```

Not every component needs every state visually, but its behavior must be explicitly defined.

---

# 34. Focus Tokens

Focus must be visible and accessible.

Conceptual tokens:

```text
focus.ring.color
focus.ring.width
focus.ring.offset
```

The focus treatment must have sufficient contrast and must not rely only on subtle color change.

Keyboard focus is a first-class state.

---

# 35. Disabled State

Disabled controls should communicate:

- unavailable
- non-interactive
- visually reduced emphasis

Do not rely on opacity alone.

Disabled controls must not appear identical to enabled controls.

---

# 36. Loading Tokens

Loading should communicate actual state.

Use:

- skeletons for page/data loading
- inline spinners for short actions
- progress indicators for measurable work
- execution state indicators for AI/background jobs

Never simulate progress that is not known.

---

# 37. Status Tokens

Canonical status semantics should remain consistent.

Example mapping:

| Semantic state | Visual role |
|---|---|
| Planned | neutral |
| In Progress | accent/info |
| Completed | success |
| At Risk | warning |
| Blocked | danger |
| Failed | danger |
| Pending Review | warning/info |
| Generating | AI/accent |
| Validating | AI/info |
| Approved | success |

The exact component representation may differ, but the semantic meaning must not.

---

# 38. AI State Tokens

AI workflows need explicit states:

```text
ai.queued
ai.running
ai.streaming
ai.validating
ai.reviewing
ai.completed
ai.failed
ai.retrying
ai.cancelled
```

Visual treatment must clearly distinguish:

- model generation
- validation
- QA/Judge
- persisted result

The interface must never imply that generated text is canonical merely because it is displayed.

---

# 39. Data Visualization Tokens

GrowFlow dashboards are visual data instruments.

Charts should use a restrained data palette.

Rules:

1. Use semantic color when the series has semantic meaning.
2. Use neutral variations when color is only categorical.
3. Avoid rainbow palettes.
4. Ensure sufficient contrast.
5. Do not use color as the only encoding.
6. Include labels/tooltips where appropriate.
7. Avoid decorative chart elements.
8. Preserve consistent series meaning across dashboards.

Primary data emphasis may use the brand accent.

Semantic risk/success/warning data may use the corresponding semantic colors.

---

# 40. Tables and Dense Data

Tables are integrated into the interface rather than floating inside unnecessary card containers.

Tokens/rules should support:

```text
table.header
table.row
table.rowHover
table.rowSelected
table.border
table.compactRow
table.standardRow
```

Typical row density:

```text
compact: ~36px
standard: ~44px
comfortable: ~52px
```

Dense tables must remain readable.

---

# 41. Document Tokens

Documents and Markdown views should use:

- readable line length
- strong heading hierarchy
- comfortable paragraph spacing
- clear code blocks
- restrained borders
- consistent inline code treatment
- accessible links
- predictable table rendering

Document surfaces should feel integrated with GrowFlow rather than like a separate application.

