---

# 51. Avatar Tokens

Standard sizes:

```text
avatar.xs = 24px
avatar.s = 32px
avatar.m = 40px
avatar.l = 48px
avatar.xl = 64px
```

Use consistent fallback behavior.

Do not encode role identity through unrelated avatar colors.

---

# 52. Progress Tokens

Progress must distinguish:

- deterministic percentage
- stage progress
- indeterminate processing

Never show fake numerical percentages for AI operations when actual progress is unavailable.

For workflow stages, prefer:

```text
Queued
Running
Validating
Reviewing
Completed
```

when numeric progress cannot be truthfully calculated.

---

# 53. Lifecycle Visualization Tokens

GrowFlow's canonical lifecycle:

```text
IDEA
→ ASSESSMENT
→ BLUEPRINT
→ PLANNING
→ IMPLEMENTATION
→ TESTING
→ DEPLOYMENT
→ COMPLETED
```

The token system should support consistent lifecycle visualization across:

- project overview
- roadmap
- dashboards
- mentor views
- admin observability

---

# 54. Task / Milestone / Progress / Phase / Health

These concepts must not collapse into one generic status.

```text
Task
    ↓
Milestone
    ↓
Progress
    ↓
Phase
    ↓
Health
```

Token semantics should allow each to have independent visual representation.

Example:

- task status: completion state
- milestone status: grouped delivery state
- progress: measurable advancement
- phase: lifecycle stage
- health: risk/condition

---

# 55. Accessibility Tokens

Accessibility must be encoded into the system, not handled only during final QA.

Required token considerations:

- text contrast
- focus visibility
- touch target size
- readable font size
- spacing between controls
- reduced motion
- error-state clarity
- non-color status indicators

Do not rely solely on:

- color
- animation
- icon shape
- position

to communicate critical meaning.

---

# 56. Responsive Token Strategy

Do not duplicate the entire token system for each breakpoint.

Instead:

```text
Canonical Token
    ↓
Responsive Override Only When Needed
```

Examples:

```text
page.gutter
```

may map to:

```text
desktop = 40px
tablet = 28px
mobile = 16px
```

Typography should generally scale only where required by usability.

---

# 57. Theme Architecture

GrowFlow supports:

```text
light
dark
```

The two themes share:

- typography
- spacing
- radius
- component geometry
- information hierarchy
- motion language
- interaction behavior

They primarily differ in:

- surfaces
- text colors
- borders
- accent contrast
- shadows/elevation adaptation

Dark mode is **not** a simple inversion of the light palette.

---

# 58. Theme Switching

Theme changes must:

- preserve current page
- preserve user state
- avoid destructive reloads
- transition smoothly where appropriate
- respect system preference if product settings permit
- remain accessible

Theme preference belongs to UI/preferences state, not canonical project state.

---

# 59. CSS Implementation Model

Recommended structure:

```text
tokens/
├── primitives.css
├── semantic.css
├── themes.css
├── components.css
└── motion.css
```

Example:

```css
:root {
  --gf-color-bg-canvas: #F7F7F4;
  --gf-color-surface-default: #FFFFFF;
  --gf-color-text-primary: #1C1C1A;
  --gf-color-accent-default: #6F7F63;
  --gf-space-m: 16px;
  --gf-radius-m: 8px;
}
```

Dark theme should override semantic variables rather than requiring every component to understand dark-mode primitives.

---

# 60. Recommended CSS Consumption

Prefer:

```css
background: var(--gf-color-surface-default);
color: var(--gf-color-text-primary);
padding: var(--gf-space-m);
border-radius: var(--gf-radius-m);
```

Avoid:

```css
background: #ffffff;
padding: 17px;
border-radius: 9px;
```

inside reusable components.

Hardcoded values should be rare and justified.

---

# 61. TypeScript Token Consumption

If the frontend uses a typed token object, keep semantic categories explicit.

Conceptual structure:

```text
tokens
├── color
├── typography
├── spacing
├── radius
├── elevation
├── sizing
├── layout
├── motion
├── state
├── ai
└── component
```

Types should prevent accidental use of unrelated token categories where practical.

---

# 62. Component Token Resolution

Preferred hierarchy:

```text
Component
    ↓
Component Token
    ↓
Semantic Token
    ↓
Primitive Token
```

Example:

```text
Primary Button background
    ↓
action.primary
    ↓
accent.default
    ↓
#6F7F63
```

This makes future controlled theme changes possible without editing every component.

---

# 63. Stitch Mapping

Stitch should receive the design-token system through the compact Stitch design package.

Stitch-facing instructions should emphasize:

- Manrope
- warm light base
- charcoal dark counterpart
- muted olive accent
- restrained semantic colors
- quiet cards
- integrated dense tables
- 8px primary control radius
- 248px sidebar
- 64px authenticated top navigation
- Soft Intelligence
- subtle physical motion
- no role-specific themes
- deeper but restrained AI layer

Page-specific prompts should reference the canonical design system rather than redefining tokens.

---

# 64. Stitch Asset Priority

When supplying assets to Stitch, prioritize:

1. Logo/brand mark.
2. Manrope font.
3. Design reference imagery.
4. Token summary.
5. Component reference examples.
6. Page-specific requirements.

The complete architecture documentation remains in the GrowFlow project documentation and should not be duplicated into every Stitch prompt.

---

# 65. Public Website Token Behavior

Public pages may use:

- larger typography
- larger spacing
- editorial imagery
- stronger visual compositions
- more generous surfaces

But they must still use:

- GrowFlow colors
- Manrope
- radius system
- spacing system
- motion system
- brand mark
- semantic states

Public expressive design is an extension of the system, not a separate design system.

---

# 66. Authenticated Workplace Token Behavior

Authenticated pages prioritize:

- information density
- clarity
- productivity
- predictable controls
- integrated tables
- strong status hierarchy
- restrained decoration

Dashboard and operational pages should use fewer decorative surfaces than public marketing pages.

---

# 67. Role Consistency

The following must remain shared:

```text
BUILD
SUPERVISE
GOVERN
```

All roles use:

- same typography
- same color foundation
- same controls
- same shell principles
- same interaction states
- same accessibility rules
- same motion system

Role differentiation is contextual rather than chromatic.

---

# 68. Brand Asset Integration

The accepted Phase 06B brand direction is:

> **Connected Flow + Abstract G**

The visual mark should represent:

- growth
- flow
- progression
- structured development

It should not introduce:

- literal AI brains
- circuit boards
- neon effects
- generic leaf icons
- unrelated gradients

The token system supports the brand primarily through the restrained olive accent and neutral foundation.

---

# 69. Component Geometry Rules

Component geometry should normally use the shared scale.

Preferred values:

```text
4
6
8
12
16
20
24
32
40
48
64
```

A component may require an exception, but exceptions should be documented in the component specification.

---

# 70. Density Modes

Some application components may support:

```text
compact
standard
comfortable
```

Density should change:

- row height
- padding
- spacing

It should not arbitrarily change:

- typography hierarchy
- brand colors
- interaction semantics

---

# 71. Empty State Tokens

Empty states should use:

- quiet surface
- readable heading
- concise explanation
- one primary action when appropriate
- optional contextual illustration

Avoid oversized decorative empty-state art in dense operational areas.

---

# 72. Error State Tokens

Error states must distinguish:

```text
validation error
permission error
network error
backend error
AI error
processing failure
```

The visual system should communicate severity without creating visual panic.

Critical failures may use danger emphasis, but not excessive red coverage.

---

# 73. Success State Tokens

Success should communicate completion without overwhelming the interface.

Use:

- success color
- check/status icon
- concise message
- optional next action

Do not turn every successful mutation into a large celebratory animation.

---

# 74. Warning / Risk Tokens

GrowFlow has explicit risk concepts.

Risk presentation should use:

- warning/danger semantic colors
- textual severity
- clear action
- contextual explanation

Do not use color alone to communicate project risk.

---

# 75. Search and Command Interface Tokens

Global search should support:

```text
search.trigger
search.input
search.result
search.resultHover
search.resultSelected
search.section
search.shortcut
```

Search selection must have visible keyboard focus.

---

# 76. Notification Tokens

Notifications should support:

```text
unread
read
priority
success
warning
danger
info
AI
```

Unread state should not depend only on color; use weight, indicator, or background difference.

---

# 77. Activity Tokens

Activity is a derived chronological representation.

Activity items should support:

- timestamp
- actor
- action
- resource
- status
- optional detail

The token system should keep activity visually quieter than primary workflow state.

---

# 78. Permission-Related UI Tokens

Permission UI may need states:

```text
visible
restricted
disabled
not-actionable
```

Do not use decorative color to imply authorization.

The backend remains authoritative for authorization.

---

# 79. Security-Sensitive Visual Rules

Sensitive operations should use:

- explicit labels
- clear confirmation
- danger styling where appropriate
- visible scope
- no ambiguous icon-only actions

Admin and investigation screens must favor clarity over decorative polish.

---

# 80. Performance Considerations

Tokens should be implemented so they do not create unnecessary runtime cost.

Prefer:

- CSS variables
- static token maps
- reusable class utilities
- shared component styles

Avoid:

- per-render style object generation where unnecessary
- huge duplicated style definitions
- unnecessary animation calculations
- large visual assets for simple UI elements

---

# 81. Token Governance

A new token should only be introduced when:

1. Existing tokens cannot represent the requirement.
2. The requirement appears more than once or is a legitimate system-level semantic.
3. The name communicates meaning.
4. Light/dark behavior is understood.
5. Accessibility impact is considered.
6. Component impact is documented.

---

# 82. Token Change Process

Proposed change:

```text
Need Identified
    ↓
Check Existing Token
    ↓
Determine Semantic Need
    ↓
Propose Token
    ↓
Review
    ↓
Update Token Specification
    ↓
Update Implementation
    ↓
Validate Components
    ↓
Validate Pages
```

No silent token changes.

---

# 83. Token Versioning

If a token changes meaning, treat it as a design-system change.

Changes should be categorized:

```text
PATCH
non-semantic correction

MINOR
new compatible token

MAJOR
meaning/value change that can materially affect existing UI
```

The exact release/version mechanism can be aligned with frontend repository practices.

---

# 84. Validation Checklist

Every implementation should verify:

### Color

- [ ] Semantic colors are used.
- [ ] Light theme works.
- [ ] Dark theme works.
- [ ] Contrast is acceptable.
- [ ] No arbitrary brand colors were introduced.

### Typography

- [ ] Manrope is used.
- [ ] Approved weights are used.
- [ ] Hierarchy is consistent.
- [ ] Metadata remains readable.

### Spacing

- [ ] Approved spacing scale is used.
- [ ] Dense screens remain usable.
- [ ] Mobile gutters are correct.

### Geometry

- [ ] Approved radii are used.
- [ ] Elevation is restrained.
- [ ] Controls have correct dimensions.

### Motion

- [ ] Motion is purposeful.
- [ ] Reduced motion works.
- [ ] No decorative perpetual animation exists.

### States

- [ ] Hover exists where relevant.
- [ ] Focus exists.
- [ ] Disabled is clear.
- [ ] Loading is truthful.
- [ ] Error/success states are defined.

### Accessibility

- [ ] Keyboard navigation works.
- [ ] Focus is visible.
- [ ] Touch targets are sufficient.
- [ ] Color is not the only semantic signal.

---

# 85. Prohibited Token Patterns

Never introduce:

```text
color.ai.neon
color.role.student
color.role.mentor
color.role.admin
color.random
shadow.glow
gradient.ai
gradient.role.student
```

unless the design system is explicitly revised and frozen again.

The role-specific examples above are deliberately prohibited because GrowFlow uses one unified visual identity.

---

# 86. Anti-Pattern Examples

Do not create:

```text
Student = green
Mentor = blue
Admin = purple
AI = neon purple
```

Correct approach:

```text
All roles
    ↓
Same design system
    ↓
Different information/context
```

---

# 87. Page-Level Token Usage

A page specification may select semantic tokens but should not redefine the global system.

Example:

```text
Project Overview
    ↓
background.canvas
surface.default
text.primary
text.secondary
action.primary
status.warning
```

It should not create:

```text
projectOverviewGreen
projectOverviewDarkGray
projectOverviewCustomRadius
```

---

# 88. Workflow-Level Token Usage

Long-running workflows should combine:

- state tokens
- progress tokens
- status tokens
- AI tokens
- notification tokens

Example:

```text
Blueprint Generation
    ↓
ai.running
    ↓
ai.validating
    ↓
ai.reviewing
    ↓
status.success
```

The frontend should reflect the actual backend workflow state.

---

# 89. Relationship to Backend Architecture

Design tokens are presentation-layer infrastructure.

They must never become a source of canonical business truth.

For example:

```text
status.success
```

is a visual representation.

The backend remains responsible for:

- lifecycle state
- task state
- milestone state
- health
- permissions
- AI execution state
- canonical project data

---

# 90. Relationship to State Architecture

Tokens consume state; they do not own it.

```text
SERVER STATE
    ↓
semantic UI representation
    ↓
tokens
    ↓
component
```

Streaming state can temporarily influence visual representation:

```text
SSE event
    ↓
streaming state
    ↓
AI status token
    ↓
AI execution component
```

The eventual canonical state must reconcile through server state.

---

